/1879256784326557696#1"},{"title":"Academic Research Reading: Personalised Research Discovery - R Discovery - Articles published on components-of-vehicles","url":"https://discovery.researcher.life/topic/components-of-vehicles/21282321?page=5#1"},{"title":"Search - Audrey Demafo; Abebe Geletu; Pu Li","url":"https://sciprofiles.com/search/publications/0/default/desc?search_form%5Btext%5D=Temperature%20Maxima#2"},{"title":"Experimental study on battery thermal management of CPCM coupled with micro-grooves flat heat pipe at low temperature","url":"https://www.mendeley.com/catalogue/e9209454-8e81-39ba-bd10-0c1cb6c47bff/"}]  # Phase Change Materials for EV Battery Thermal Management: 60 °C Maximum Temperature (2024–2025)  ## The 60 °C Threshold in EV Batteries  Multiple 2024–2025 sources confirm that **60 °C is the critical maximum temperature** for lithium-ion battery (LIB) safety and performance. The optimal operating range is typically **–20 °C to 60 °C**, with temperatures exceeding 60 °C leading to cycle life loss or thermal runaway. Temperature uniformity across cells is also crucial, with gradients exceeding **5 °C** compromising stability and performance.  ## Key 2024–2025 Research Findings  ### 1. Ester-Based PCMs via PCM Immersion (Advanced Science, 2025) Geng et al. synthesized seven ester-based PCMs by reacting lauric acid with different alcohols. These achieved: - Melting temperature range: **–8.99 to 46.60 °C** (203% broader than raw alcohol materials) - Maximum latent heat: **182.98 J g⁻¹** - Using a PCM immersion technique, the battery was maintained **below 60 °C for 20.5 hours**, versus the blank group reaching 60 °C in just 0.82 h and 75 °C within 2.35 h (DOI: 10.1002/advs.202413703).  ### 2. Biomass-Derived Carbon-Enhanced Composite PCM (Journal of Energy Storage, 2025) A paraffin wax composite PCM using Undaria pinnatifida-derived carbon and expanded graphite achieved: - Thermal conductivity of **0.89 W/(m·K)** — 4.24 times pure paraffin - Reduced peak battery temperature from **77.7 °C to 59.7 °C** at 3C discharge (below the 60 °C limit) - Maintained **temperature uniformity within 5 °C** - Also prevented melting leakage via the porous carbon framework  ### 3. Micro-Encapsulated PCM Coatings (ECS Meeting Abstracts, July 2025) Quarrell et al. encapsulated octadecane in graphene oxide shells (~2 µm capsules), applied as a ~3 mm coating on 18650 cells: - Latent heat of ~100 J g⁻¹ - Reduced peak cell temperature by **8 °C during 2C fast charging** - Required only 13% mass increase - PCMs generally store 150–250 J g⁻¹ and can keep cells **below 50 °C** under fast charging  ### 4. Graphene-Enhanced PCMs (Review, Chapter 1228340) Graphene-enhanced composite PCMs demonstrate: - PCM-fin arrangements can keep maximum temperature **below 60 °C**; hybrid PCM with bottom liquid cooling keeps it below 50 °C - Graphene-coated nickel foam with paraffin reduced temperature rise by 17% - 6-mm PCM plates reduced surface temperature from 56.5 to 36.5 °C at 4C discharge - Nano-enhanced PCMs showed stability over **3,000 thermal cycles**; functionalized graphene PCMs remained stable after 500 cycles  ### 5. CPCM Coupled with Flat Heat Pipe (Applied Thermal Engineering, 2025) At **5C discharge**, a composite PCM + micro-groove flat heat pipe system kept battery maximum temperature **below 45 °C**, versus **>60 °C** without thermal management. The system also provided low-temperature insulation, holding batteries above 0 °C for 3+ hours in –20 °C environments.  ## Summary  Recent (2024–2025) research confirms that PCM-based battery thermal management systems can effectively maintain EV lithium-ion battery temperatures **below the 60 °C safety threshold**, even at high discharge rates (3C–5C). The most promising approaches involve composite PCMs with enhanced thermal conductivity (via biomass carbon, expanded graphite, or graphene), micro-encapsulation to prevent leakage, and hybrid configurations (PCM plus heat pipes or liquid cooling) that achieve both temperature suppression and uniformity improvements.   REMINDER: You MUST include the sources above in your response to the user using markdown hyperlinks.
