[tool_result]Web search results for query: "advanced thermal management systems next-generation EV batteries liquid cooling PCM heat pipe 4C fast charging 2024 2025"  Links: [{"title":"Advances in battery thermal management systems and energy storage technologies - Skip to main contentSkip to article","url":"https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/abs/pii/S0735193326005713?dgcid=rss_sd_all#1"},{"title":"Hybrid phase change material techniques for battery thermal management in electric vehicles: A comprehensive review and bibliometric analysis - Jilte et al (Jilte et al","url":"https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/S2352484725006390?fr=RR-2&ref=pdf_download&rr=a10dfd30fe60f50e#6"},{"title":"Global advancement, prospect and challenges of battery thermal management systems: A comprehensive review - Skip to main contentSkip to article","url":"https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/abs/pii/S1364032125005969#1"},{"title":"Hybrid fuzzy-MPC based multi-objective control strategy for fast charging of electric vehicles with advanced battery thermal management and renewable grid support - Battery Temperature Management Systems (BTMS) commonly utilize cooling methods such as phase change materials (PCMs), heat pipes...","url":"https://www.nature.com/articles/s41598-025-26279-4#content#4"},{"title":"New concept of hybrid cooling system for fast charging of electric vehicles","url":"https://researchportal.vub.be/en/publications/new-concept-of-hybrid-cooling-system-for-fast-charging-of-electri/"},{"title":"Advancements and challenges in battery thermal management technologies for electric vehicles; a systematic navigation on the latest trends","url":"https://www.mendeley.com/catalogue/79106c89-aafc-3ef2-bed2-765aedb770bc/"},{"title":"New concept of hybrid cooling system for fast charging of electric vehicles","url":"https://scholar-cnki-net-443.webvpn.imac.edu.cn/zn/Detail/index/GARJ2021_5/SJESAB3C9B0BDD9DA953009A76740714AFA5"},{"title":"Numerical Improvement of Battery Thermal Management Integrating Phase Change Materials with Fin-Enhanced Liquid Cooling.","url":"https://fub-hagen.digibib.net/search/eds/record/enr:185128341?be-eds-facetfilter=1%2CContentProvider%3AEnergy+%26+Power+Source&be-eds-sort=relevance&count=20&q-ky=%22thermal%22&start=1&pos=2"},{"title":"Advancements and challenges in battery thermal management technologies for electric vehicles; a systematic navigation on the latest trends - Advancements and challenges in battery thermal management technologies for electric vehicles; a systematic navigation on the lat...","url":"https://www.academia.edu/143458174/Advancements_and_challenges_in_battery_thermal_management_technologies_for_electric_vehicles_a_systematic_navigation_on_the_latest_trends#1"},{"title":"Nanofluid-Based Cooling Strategies for Intelligent BTMSs in Electric Vehicles: Recent Advances, Thermal Safety, and Control-Oriented Architectures","url":"https://www.mendeley.com/catalogue/652dfcb9-831e-3b8f-9751-573bbbcc772e/"}]  # Advanced Thermal Management for Next-Generation EV Batteries (2024–2025)  ## Overview Battery thermal management systems (BTMS) are increasingly recognized as critical for EV battery safety, lifespan, and driver trust, especially as fast and ultra-fast charging demands grow. Lithium-ion batteries operate optimally within a narrow **20–40 °C range**, with deviations significantly affecting capacity, safety, and lifetime. Recent reviews systematically categorize BTMS into **active** methods (air, liquid, immersion cooling) and **passive** methods (phase change materials/PCMs, heat pipes), with a strong consensus that **hybrid approaches combining multiple technologies** are necessary to meet next-generation requirements, as no single method suffices under high-current fast-charging conditions.  ## Key Technologies  **Liquid cooling** remains the mainstream solution in modern EVs due to its scalability, though cost efficiency still requires improvement. Air cooling is simple but relatively inefficient for heat extraction, particularly natural convection.  **Phase change materials (PCMs)** have emerged as viable passive solutions, especially in hybrid BTMS configurations. Their low thermal conductivity is the primary limitation, addressed through metal foams, nanoparticles, and fin-enhanced structures. PCM and air-based systems offer higher scalability but suffer reduced cooling capacity at larger battery module sizes.  **Heat pipes** show promise for scalability, but further work on their integration with battery cells is needed. Nanofluid-enhanced PCM, nanofluid-based heat pipe cooling, and nanofluid indirect liquid cooling are all being investigated, with **hybrid and ternary nanofluids** demonstrating superior thermal performance and suppression of safety-critical temperatures.  ## Hybrid System Performance at 4C Fast Charging  A landmark 2024 study (Behi et al., *Journal of Energy Storage*) validated a novel hybrid TMS combining **aluminum plate, PCM heat buffer, and liquid cooling plates** for LTO battery modules under fast charging:  | Cooling method | 4C charging max temp | 8C charging max temp | |---|---|---| | Natural convection only | 42 °C | 58.5 °C | | With PCM heat buffer | 36.3 °C (−13.5%) | 49 °C (−16%) | | Full hybrid (PCM + liquid) | **31.2 °C (−25.7%)** | **38.4 °C (−34.3%)** |  Natural convection alone failed to maintain acceptable temperatures during fast charging.  A 2025 numerical study in *Energies* found that coupling PCM with fin-enhanced liquid cooling effectively controls pack temperatures during **4C discharge (8 A)**. Merely adding cooling plates and PCM filling achieved only a 1.8 K peak temperature reduction and exceeded 10 K temperature difference. However, optimized flow-channel configurations in fins, alternating inlet/outlet arrangements, coolant flow at 0.5 m/s, and 293.15 K inlet temperature maintained packs **below 306 K with ~5 K maximum temperature difference**. Notably, flow rates beyond 0.7 m/s yielded negligible improvement due to inherent conductivity limits of battery/PCM materials.  ## Intelligent Control and AI Integration  2024–2025 research increasingly integrates **machine learning and model predictive control (MPC)** into BTMS: - Artificial neural networks can predict cell surface temperatures with high accuracy, avoiding full PDE-based thermal models. - A hybrid fuzzy-MPC strategy for fast charging achieved **97–98% final state of charge** vs. 91–93% for fuzzy logic alone, while maintaining lower battery temperatures (37–40 °C vs. 42–43 °C). - This approach reduced capacity fade to 5.2% (a 58% improvement over conventional charging) and extended projected cycle life to ~1500 cycles. - Adaptive optimization strategies (e.g., Pontryagin's Minimal Principle-based control) dynamically adjust to driving and environmental conditions.  ## Challenges and Future Directions  - **Scalability and manufacturing**: Hybrid mini-channel PCM systems face fabrication complexity and clogging issues; PCM-hybrid systems need simplified manufacturing and Design-for-Manufacturing-and-Assembly (DFMA) approaches for industrial adoption. - **Material stability**: Long-term thermal cycling (500–1000 cycles) studies are needed on particle sedimentation and nanoparticle distribution in PCM composites, using X-ray tomography and SEM. - **Mechanical vibration**: Road-induced vibrations (10–50 Hz) can weaken PCM-hybrid reliability and require controlled real-world-condition experiments. - **Thermoeconomic analysis**: Life-cycle cost analysis (LCCA), Net Present Value, and Levelized Cost of Storage are needed to validate long-term cost-effectiveness of PCM enhancements. - **Research gaps**: Most studies evaluate cooling under controlled lab conditions rather than dynamic driving environments, limiting real-world reliability; future systems must be **intelligent, adaptive, climate-aware, and safety-integrated**, moving from conventional cooling toward multifunctional thermal-safety-energy management platforms. - **Simulation capabilities**: Multi-physics frameworks coupling electrochemical-thermal-fluid dynamics now capture ion transport, Joule heating in current collectors, and coolant heat transfer dynamics, while reduced-order models enable real-time control integration with vehicle energy management systems.   REMINDER: You MUST include the sources above in your response to the user using markdown hyperlinks.
