rsion cooling of a four cell lithium-ion battery module","url":"https://discovery.researcher.life/article/an-experimental-investigation-of-liquid-immersion-cooling-of-a-four-cell-lithium-ion-battery-module/99c71fbee2d7303da57904a286aaeef4#1"},{"title":"An experimental investigation of liquid immersion cooling of a four cell lithium-ion battery module - An experimental investigation of liquid immersion cooling of a four cell lithium-ion battery module","url":"https://www.ablesci.com/scholar/paper?id=rnPlzVmXE#1"}]  # Immersion Cooling for Battery Thermal Management at 4C Fast Charging: 2024–2025 Research Summary  ## Overview Recent research (2024–2025) has established immersion cooling as a highly effective battery thermal management strategy for 4C fast charging, where battery packs are submerged directly in dielectric fluids. Key studies include a 2025 simulation of immersion-cooled battery pack performance at 4C fast charging (published in *Automotive and New Power*), covering system structure design, flow channel design, coolant selection, and cell spatial layout strategies.  ## Dielectric Fluid Performance Multiple dielectric fluids have been evaluated for single-phase immersion cooling:  - **Deionized water** provided the best cooling performance, limiting temperature difference (ΔT) to 0.26 °C at 3C with only 0.05 kg/s flow. - **Mineral oil** and **AmpCool AC-100** were also compared; mineral oil offered better thermal uniformity than therminol oil, while synthetic ester oil and forced-flow systems achieved maximum temperature drops of nearly 30% at higher Reynolds numbers. - **Vegetable oil** was studied as a biodegradable alternative to fossil fuel-based coolants for an 8S3P battery module at high discharge rates (4C, 6C, and 8C), with coolant selection based on cooling efficiency, power consumption, and temperature uniformity. Flow rate significantly affected coolant selection.  ## Two-Phase (Phase-Change) Cooling Results Two-phase immersion cooling—where low-boiling-point dielectric fluids leverage latent heat—delivered the most impressive 4C results:  - A 2024 experimental study (Williams et al., *Journal of Energy Storage*) using **Novec 7000** on four 2.5 Ah 26650 LiFePO4 cells found that at **4C fast charging**, pool boiling limited the maximum temperature rise to **1.3 °C** (max temperature 35 °C), and at 4C discharge to **1.9 °C** (max 34.7 °C). Module thermal homogeneity was excellent, with a maximum ΔT of just **1.2 °C**; vigorous boiling at cell electrodes enhanced heat transfer at high heat flux locations. - **SF33** dielectric fluid kept peak temperatures under **35 °C** during charging and reduced Tmax by 12 °C during discharging; another study confirmed a 10.7 °C Tmax reduction with SF33. - **FS49** achieved substantial improvements in both temperature reduction and uniformity versus air cooling. - **Propane and R134a** as working fluids delivered peak temperature reductions exceeding **10 °C** compared to non-boiling setups.  ## Hybrid and Novel Approaches - **Jet impingement combined with immersion**: CFD optimization with six 3 mm jet pipes and a 2:1 flow split cut Tmax from 39.1 °C to **28.8 °C** with ΔT of 3.8 K. Jet impingement outperformed static immersion, especially at high C-rates (mineral oil nearly maintained uniform cell skin temperature at 2C charge/3C discharge). - **Bionic flow guides**: Fish-shaped holes reduced Tmax by **12.2%** and pump power by **42.1%**. - **Self-organizing manifold flow with checkerboard layout**: module temperature difference dropped by **43.8%** (ΔT of 3.86 K).  ## Neural Network Prediction (2025) A 2025 study in the *International Journal of Thermal Sciences* used artificial neural networks to predict cell temperatures in immersion cooling systems using mineral oil and an engineered fluid for 32700 LiFePO4 cells. At 0.01 kg/s mass flow, average temperature rise stayed **below 5 °C at 3C** and **below 10 °C at 4C** discharge for both fluids. The ANN model achieved maximum prediction error of less than 1 K, identifying flow rate and coolant selection as the most critical optimization parameters.  ## Key Takeaways for 4C Fast Charging 1. **Two-phase immersion cooling** with low-boiling dielectric fluids (e.g., Novec 7000, SF33) is the most effective approach, keeping temperatures near 35 °C during 4C charging with ΔT under 1.2 °C. 2. **Single-phase cooling** with optimized flow and fluid selection can maintain temperature rises under 10 °C at 4C. 3. **Coolant type, flow rate, and cell spacing** are the dominant design variables; higher flow rates reduce thermal gradients but increase pressure drop and pumping power.   REMINDER: You MUST include the sources above in your response to the user using markdown hyperlinks.
