[tool_result]Web search results for query: "nanofluid cooling battery thermal management electric vehicle 2024 2025 performance comparison"  Links: [{"title":"Silver nanoparticle-based nanofluid vs. conventional cooling approach: a comparative study on thermal management in lithium-ion battery packs - Silver nanoparticle-based nanofluid vs. conventional cooling approach: a comparative study on thermal management in lithium-ion ...","url":"https://link.springer.com/article/10.1007/s43995-025-00173-6#1"},{"title":"Thermal management of high-discharge lithium-ion prismatic cells using various dielectric nanofluid-based novel immersion cooling design","url":"https://link-hkg.springer.com/article/10.1007/s10973-025-14040-y"},{"title":"Thermal management strategies for a prismatic Li-ion batteryfor phase change material and hybrid nanofluid coolantsusing NTGK Model - Your download is now in progress and you may close this window","url":"https://www.tandfonline.com/doi/full/10.1080/01430750.2025.2556770?scroll=top&needAccess=true#1"},{"title":"Silver nanoparticle-based nanofluid vs. conventional cooling approach: a comparative study on thermal management in lithium-ion ...","url":"https://link-proxy.springer.com/content/pdf/10.1007/s43995-025-00173-6.pdf#5#1"},{"title":"Performance investigation of mono and hybrid nanofluids in a liquid battery thermal management system under high discharge rate and different drive cycle conditions - Journal|[J]Journal of Energy StorageVolume 116, 2025. PP 116084-116084国际期刊","url":"https://scholar-cnki-net-443.webvpn.imac.edu.cn/zn/Detail/index/GARJ2021_5/SJES765F7222D1E74B440BCB694697DB40CC#1"},{"title":"Towards Efficient and Scalable Battery Thermal Management in Electric Vehicles: A Review of Technologies, Challenges, and Emerging Solutions","url":"https://xplorestaging.ieee.org/document/11212315"},{"title":"Li-Ion Battery Cooling and Heating System with Loop Thermosyphon for Electric Vehicles – DOAJ","url":"https://doaj.org/article/d9ae356156e2470d84e1dc709f4fb98e"},{"title":"Li-Ion Battery Cooling and Heating System with Loop Thermosyphon for Electric Vehicles. - Test Site: Library Search","url":"https://libcattest.canterbury.ac.nz/EdsRecord/a9h,186930434?sid=2688161#fulltext#1"},{"title":"Li-Ion Battery Cooling and Heating System with Loop Thermosyphon for Electric Vehicles - Li-Ion Battery Cooling and Heating System with Loop Thermosyphon for Electric Vehicles","url":"https://nufind.nu.edu.sa/EdsRecord/edsdoj,edsdoj.9ae356156e2470d84e1dc709f4fb98e#1"},{"title":"Performance investigation of mono and hybrid nanofluids in a liquid battery thermal management system under high discharge rate and different drive cycle conditions - Skip to main contentSkip to article","url":"https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/abs/pii/S2352152X25007972#1"}]  # Nanofluid Cooling for EV Battery Thermal Management: 2024–2025 Performance Comparisons  ## Overview  Recent (2024–2025) research confirms that nanofluid-based cooling is a promising approach for electric vehicle (EV) battery thermal management systems (BTMS), but results vary significantly by nanoparticle type, concentration, and system architecture. No single fluid universally outperforms all others, and most studies indicate that hybrid and nanofluid-based systems hold "vast potential" for high-performance thermal management (IEEE Xplore review, 2025).  ## Key Performance Comparisons from Recent Studies  ### Nanofluids vs. Conventional Coolants - **Silver (Ag) nanoparticle nanofluids vs. water vs. air** (Springer, 2025): A study on a 24-cell 18650 lithium-ion battery pack compared Ag nanofluids at 0.25% and 0.50% volume fractions (water-based), pure water, and forced air cooling. Silver was chosen for its exceptional thermal conductivity (429 W/mK) and improved stability (reduced agglomeration/sedimentation). Water-based liquid cooling was implemented after validating an air-cooling model against literature; the study also analyzed performance under varying ambient temperatures and coolant flow velocities. This work specifically responds to the identified gap that "comprehensive comparative analyses with traditional air and liquid cooling systems are necessary." - **PCM vs. nanofluids vs. synthetic ester oil vs. water** (Taylor & Francis, 2025): Using the NTGK model on a prismatic Li-ion battery across heat transfer coefficients of 5–20 W/m²K, **N-Octadecane (phase change material) outperformed all fluids by 35–40%**, surpassing synthetic ester oil by 66.5% under non-flow conditions. It maintained the lowest maximum cell temperature (304.987 K) with a minimal temperature difference of 0.684°C. Hybrid and ternary hybrid nanofluids showed enhanced heat transfer efficiency, while water provided effective heat distribution due to high specific heat capacity; synthetic ester oil lagged despite strong electrical insulation. - **Loop thermosyphon system** (Energies, July 2025): Testing water, acetone, and TiO₂/nano-silver water (NSW) nanofluids across inclinations (0–60°) and ambient temperatures (−10 to 20°C), **pure water proved to be the most effective working fluid**. One configuration maintained cell temperatures at 50.4°C with water vs. 31.6°C with acetone under a 50 W heat load; another achieved 41.8°C with water and 42.8°C with 0.01 vol% TiO₂ nanofluid. Critically, **performance deteriorated at higher nanofluid concentrations due to increased viscosity** and thermophysical constraints.  ### Nanofluid Type Comparisons - **SWCNT water nanofluid (best overall liquid nanofluid)** (Journal of Energy Storage, 2025): Testing 12- and 100-cell cylindrical modules under 3C discharge and WLTC3/NEDC drive cycles, the SWCNT water nanofluid at 3% concentration was the optimum among SWCNT, Fe₃O₄+TiO₂, and Cu+TiO₂ (mono and hybrid). Compared to water, it achieved a **3.56% reduction in maximum temperature and 13.81% reduction in temperature difference**, yielding a performance factor of 1.58 and an **80.13% enhancement in Nusselt number**. Under drive cycle conditions, it lowered max temperature by 0.5% and temperature difference by 3.3%. BTMS performance was directly proportional to Reynolds number. - **Metal oxide nanofluids** (Springer, 2025 review of prior work):   - **SiO₂ at 5 vol%** achieved the lowest average cell temperatures in a 52-cell cylindrical pack, with enhanced thermal diffusion and flow turbulence.   - **CuO** provided best thermal performance in cylindrical packs; **Al₂O₃** was most effective in square packs (Xu et al.).   - **Al₂O₃ (1–4 vol%)** in water/glycol yielded only slight reductions in maximum module temperature—minimal overall impact (Mondal et al.).   - **TiO₂** thermal conductivity increases primarily with concentration; size and shape have lesser effects.  ## Key Metrics and Trends - Optimum battery operating range: **20–40°C**; maintaining this range is the primary BTMS objective. - Performance evaluation criteria across studies: maximum temperature, temperature difference/uniformity, pressure drop, Nusselt number, performance factor (heat transfer enhancement vs. pressure drop penalty), energy consumption, response time, cost, scalability. - **Concentration effects**: Performance generally improves with nanoparticle concentration up to an optimum (e.g., 3% for SWCNT, 5% for SiO₂), but higher concentrations can hurt performance through increased viscosity—as seen with TiO₂ nanofluids in the thermosyphon system. - **Cooling mode conclusions**: The lightweight loop thermosyphon design outperformed conventional air-cooling and matched liquid-cooling performance; nanofluid systems integrate readily into existing liquid-cooling architectures without major modifications, making them scalable.  ## Remaining Challenges (Consistently Cited) - Selection of optimal nanofluid formulations - Prevention of nanoparticle agglomeration and sedimentation (long-term stability) - Evaluation under varying ambient conditions and real-world drive cycles - Higher viscosity and pressure drop penalties at elevated concentrations - Most research remains computational; experimental validation under practical conditions is costly and time-consuming - Need for rigorous head-to-head comparisons with traditional air and liquid cooling systems  ## Bottom Line Across 2024–2025 studies, **SWCNT-water nanofluids at ~3% concentration** represent the strongest liquid nanofluid option for EV BTMS (with a performance factor of 1.58 vs. water), while **PCMs like N-Octadecane deliver the best thermal regulation overall** (35–40% better than nanofluids). Silver nanoparticle nanofluids offer excellent thermal conductivity and stability but are still being validated against baseline coolants. Pure water often remains competitive or superior in practical systems, particularly where nanofluid viscosity increases offset heat transfer gains.   REMINDER: You MUST include the sources above in your response to the user using markdown hyperlinks.
