[tool_result]Web search results for query: "thermal runaway prevention battery 60 degrees celsius safety threshold fast charging next generation solid state battery 2025"  Links: [{"title":"Revealing cycling and thermal safety characteristics of LiFePO4 solid-state lithium metal batteries under dual in-situ strategy","url":"https://www.mendeley.com/catalogue/3a9c464d-f285-37a8-bf64-0495d79c1931/"},{"title":"Revealing cycling and thermal safety characteristics of LiFePO4 solid-state lithium metal batteries under dual in-situ strategy - Skip to main contentSkip to article","url":"https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/abs/pii/S2095495625000142#1"},{"title":"Balancing EV Battery Energy Density with Thermal Safety: A Complete R&D Guide (2025) - Your Agentic AI Partner","url":"https://www.patsnap.com/de/resources/blog/articles/ev-battery-energy-density-thermal-safety-guide/#1"},{"title":"固态电池技术优缺点全面总结！","url":"https://ner.jgvogel.cn/c/1546/1546031.shtml"},{"title":"新国标引领动力电池新生态","url":"https://www.kte99.com/industry/85.html"},{"title":"综述：开发安全高性能锂离子电池的策略与方法","url":"https://news.ebiotrade.com/2025-6/20250618030214798.htm"},{"title":"未来能源：固态电池技术方向多样，硫化物主流方向确定（附20页报告） - 未来能源：固态电池技术方向多样，硫化物主流方向确定（附20页报告）","url":"http://www.cheyanzixun.com/page10?article_id=65280#1"},{"title":"Équilibrer la densité énergétique des batteries électriques et la sécurité thermique : guide complet de R&D (2025) - Votre partenaire en intelligence artificielle","url":"https://www.patsnap.com/fr/resources/blog/articles/ev-battery-energy-density-thermal-safety-guide/#1"},{"title":"未来能源：固态电池技术方向多样，硫化物主流方向确定（附20页报告）","url":"https://www.sohu.com/a/952573066_121779604?scm=10001.325_13-325_13.0.0.5_32&spm=smpc.channel_248.block3_308_NDdFbm_1_fd.1.1762693804134BqtmZU1_324"},{"title":"Solid-State Batteries: The Future of Energy Storage_DC Energy","url":"https://dcebx.com/index.php?m=home&c=View&a=index&aid=94&lang=en"}]  # Thermal Runaway Prevention, the 60°C Threshold, and Next-Generation Solid-State Batteries (2025)  ## The 60°C Safety Threshold  Multiple 2025 sources converge on **60°C as the critical safety boundary** for battery operation. Below this temperature, cells operate normally; above it, risk escalates rapidly. The thermal runaway cascade is well-documented: when internal temperature exceeds ~60°C, the anode's SEI (solid-electrolyte interphase) film begins decomposing; at ~90–120°C SEI breakdown exposes lithiated carbon to electrolyte, triggering exothermic reactions and flammable gas generation; separators melt around 130–165°C; cathode oxygen release occurs near 180–200°C; and peak thermal runaway temperatures reach **400–800°C**.  Significantly, China's new mandatory standard **GB 38031-2025** (issued April 2025) codifies this threshold: after a triggered single-cell thermal runaway, the pack must show **no fire or explosion during a 2-hour observation period with all monitoring points ≤60°C**—a major escalation from the 2020 standard, which only required 5 minutes of escape time. The standard also adds mandatory fast-charge endurance testing (300 fast-charge cycles at 20–80% SOC) followed by external short-circuit testing, and paves the way for solid-state and other novel chemistries via supplementary testing provisions.  ## How Next-Generation Solid-State Batteries Prevent Thermal Runaway  **Non-flammable solid electrolytes eliminate the primary fuel source** for battery fires. Whereas liquid electrolytes have flash points around 30–40°C and decompose exothermically above 150°C, inorganic solid electrolytes are intrinsically stable: **oxide-based SSEs (LLZO) remain stable above 1000°C**, and **sulfide-based SSEs (Li₆PS₅Cl) are stable to 200–300°C**. Solid electrolytes also exhibit initial exothermic temperatures generally above 200°C, and their dense structure physically blocks lithium dendrite penetration, eliminating the internal short-circuit trigger that dominates liquid-cell failures. Heat generation during thermal runaway in SSBs is only **~20–30%** of that in conventional liquid-electrolyte batteries.  A landmark **2025 Journal of Energy Chemistry study** (LiFePO₄ solid-state lithium-metal batteries using a dual in-situ strategy) quantified these improvements: pouch cells achieved a **self-heating onset temperature (T-onset) of 180°C** and a **lower thermal runaway maximum temperature (T-max) of 401°C**, with the modified SSE generating only 9.9% of the gas of unmodified cells. The same study demonstrated **92% capacity retention after 50 cycles at 60°C**—directly validating operation at the safety threshold—and showed 31% heat-production reduction from 30 to 70°C (versus 54% for liquid batteries), indicating lower temperature sensitivity. A LiF-enriched anode layer plus Sn-based nucleation modification delivered 92% capacity retention after 550 cycles and suppressed dendrites even under 5.5V overcharge.  ## Fast Charging Considerations  SSBs theoretically enable **up to 6× faster charging** than conventional lithium-ion batteries, thanks to lithium-metal anodes and rapid ion transport. However, key challenges remain: solid-state electrolytes have lower ionic conductivity (0.1–10 mS/cm versus 10–15 mS/cm for the best liquid electrolytes), and solid–solid interfacial resistance hinders fast charging and cycling. Thermal management is also critical—well-designed liquid cooling limits pack temperature rise to <5°C with 2–3°C cell-to-cell uniformity; immersion cooling in dielectric fluid achieves **temperature uniformity within 1°C even under fast-charging conditions**; heat pipes reduce peak temperatures 20–30%; and hybrid PCM-liquid systems improve efficiency 10–15%. For 2025, the Chinese standard's new fast-charge cycling test reflects industry concern that repeated high-rate charging degrades cells and increases failure risk.  ## 2025 Status and Outlook  Solid-state batteries offer **400–500 Wh/kg cell-level energy density** versus 250–300 Wh/kg for liquid systems, and **operate at temperatures above 60°C** where traditional batteries are limited to -20 to 60°C. Lifespans of **8,000–10,000 cycles** are projected versus 1,500–2,000 for conventional cells. Sulfide-based electrolytes are widely viewed as the mainstream technical path, with CATL, BYD, Huawei, Toyota, Panasonic, Samsung SDI, LG Energy Solution, SK On, QuantumScape, and Solid Power all investing heavily. Commercial solid-state cells for premium EVs are projected for **2027–2030**, with remaining challenges including high cost, interface resistance, manufacturing complexity (high-temperature sintering, air-sensitive processing), and mechanical brittleness.   REMINDER: You MUST include the sources above in your response to the user using markdown hyperlinks.
